<section class="era" id="case-11">
  <div class="era-head"><span class="era-no">CASE 11</span><h2 style="margin:0; font-size:1.35rem;">The seven seconds between the jobs</h2><span class="era-dates">May – Aug 2026</span></div>
  <p class="impact-pill"><span class="lab">Impact</span><span class="val">wait ~118s → ≈0 · 5,000 daily</span></p>
  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The problem</span>
    <p>By mid-2026, 5,000-order runs were routine — and the daily test showed the Borrower Ownership step
    taking <strong>around two minutes</strong> for 4,000 of the 5,000 orders. The decisive clue came from an
    exercise anyone can copy, and we ran it first on the purchase step next door: add up the actual CPU time
    of every job in the step and compare it with the wall clock. The jobs came to
    <strong>521 milliseconds</strong>, inside a step that observably took <strong>seven and a half
    seconds</strong>. The same arithmetic on Borrower Ownership told the same story on a crueller scale —
    about a second of work inside two minutes of elapsed time. The code was innocent in both cases; the
    difference was time spent standing in line. And queue wait has a cruel personality: invisible when the
    system is idle (~0s), noticeable under load (~7s at 91 orders a minute), catastrophic at peak
    (~118 seconds).</p>
  </div>
