  <div class="attempt">
    <span class="tag">What we tried — and the bottleneck that moved</span>
    <p>The fix was to stop making unrelated work share one line. Ownership transfers and auto-sales had been
    competing for the same eight workers; each got its own dedicated queue on its own server, and the
    two-minute wait evaporated.</p>
    <p>Then the fix backfired — upstream. With that backpressure gone, the <em>purchase</em> step nearly
    tripled, from 3.6 to 9.9 seconds. All the throughput we had just freed slammed into a 700,000-row table
    that had <strong>no index on the one column every duplicate-check searched</strong> — a full table scan
    that had been hiding behind the queue the entire time. One index later, the job was back under a
    second.</p>
    <p>The rest was housekeeping that only matters at this scale: fifteen log lines per order deleted
    (about 100&nbsp;MB of logs a day at 5,000 orders), unused indexes dropped to cut write cost, and
    explicit connection budgets after the full worker fleet exhausted the database's connections.</p>
<div class="codebox">
  <span class="cap">database/migrations/..._add_external_order_no_index_to_local_market_orders_table.php</span>
  <pre><code>/**
 * local_market_orders has ~700k rows and no index on external_order_no, so
 * every lookup by reference (the duplicate check in CreateLocalMarketOrderAction
 * and LocalMarketTrait::getLocalMarketOrderByReference used by transfer/sell/
 * cancel) runs a full table scan. Under the perf-test burst these concurrent
 * scans thrash the buffer pool and inflate the initiate-order step. A plain
 * index turns them into single-row lookups (LYNKMRBHA-6509).
 */
public function up(): void
&#123;
    Schema::table('local_market_orders', function (Blueprint $table) &#123;
        $table-&gt;index('external_order_no', 'local_market_orders_external_order_no_index');
    &#125;);
&#125;</code></pre>
</div>

