          <td>The two highest-volume job types were blocking each other on eight shared workers.</td>
          <td>4 replicas each, on two machines. The ~118-second wait went to ~0 (Case&nbsp;11).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>trader_order_initiation</code> 5&nbsp;→&nbsp;10<br><span class="tk">Jul 2026</span></td>
          <td>Removing the downstream backpressure exposed this queue as the next constraint — jobs waiting, not working.</td>
          <td class="num">10 at rest, 20 mid-deploy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Unit-creation queue 5&nbsp;→&nbsp;8<br><span class="tk">Jul 2026</span></td>
          <td>It became the new tail of the purchase step once everything upstream got faster.</td>
          <td class="num">8 per machine at rest, 16 mid-deploy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Rebalance four queues across machines<br><span class="tk">Jul 2026</span></td>
          <td>One server carried 63 worker processes while another carried 22.</td>
          <td>Per-server steady totals 22&nbsp;/&nbsp;63&nbsp;/&nbsp;28&nbsp;→&nbsp;35&nbsp;/&nbsp;40&nbsp;/&nbsp;36 — a fleet of about 111 at that point, which the increases above and after took to today's 122. No queue's total concurrency changed; only which machine ran it.</td>
        </tr>
